

<p>
You can create scripts in Substrata using the <a href="https://luau-lang.org/">Luau programming language</a>, 
which is Roblox's version of the <a href="https://www.lua.org/">Lua programming language.</a>
</p>

<h2>Creating an object script</h2>

<p>
To create a script for your object, you will need to use the native Substrata client (i.e. not the web client).  This can be downloaded from the <a href="/">main Substrata page</a>.
</p>
<p>
	To get all the scripting functionality, you may need the latest test Substrata client, available on the <a href="https://discord.gg/R6tfYn3">Substrata discord channel</a>.
</p>
<p>
In the native client, double click on the object you want to script.
Then click on the 'Edit' button in the script section:
</p>

<img src="/files/edit_script_button.JPG" width="1000px"/>

<p>
This will pop up the script editor window (shown here with an existing script):
</p>

<img src="/files/script_editor_window.JPG" width="1000px" />

<p>
To create a Luau script, you <b>must start your script</b> with
</p>

<pre class="code-block">
--lua
</pre>

<p>
This tells Substrata to expect a Luau script, as opposed to a Winter or XML script.
</p>
<h2>Vector constructors</h2>
<p>
Use <code>Vec3d(x, y, z)</code> for an object's double-precision world position. Use <code>Vec3f(x, y, z)</code> for float-vector fields such as rotation axis, scale, material colour and avatar velocity. These are Substrata constructors available to scripts.
</p>
<pre class="code-block">
this_object.pos = Vec3d(0, 0, 2)
this_object.scale = Vec3f(1, 1, 1)
</pre>
<p>
To apply changes in your script to the object, just close the script editor window.
</p>


<h2>Script execution environment</h2>

<p>
Your script will run on both the client and the server. 
For example, that means that every client that connects to substrata.info will run your script, and your script will also run on the substrata.info server.
</p>
<p>
Calling some functions or assigning some attributes will generally only have any immediate effect on either the client or server, depending on what attribute or function it is.
</p>
<p>
Attribute updates that affect physics behaviour will generally run client-side, because physics in Substrata is computed client side.
</p>
<p>
Other attribute updates will only run on the server, and then the updated object information will be immediately and automatically sent to all connected clients.
</p>
<p>




<h2>Debugging your script</h2>

You can print output from your script with the print function, for example

<pre class="code-block">
print("hello")
</pre>

<p>
Debugging output, including print output is available in the log window (Tools > Show Log).
</p>
<p>
If an error occurs while running your script, the error while be shown in the log window (or the server log) as well, and the script will be stopped.
To restart your script you will need to edit it, thereby reloading the script.
</p>
<p>
Here is an example of a message printed from print():
</p>

<img src="/files/log_window.JPG" width="1000px" />

<p>
As well as the actual message, the UID of the script's object is also printed.

</p>
<p>
Since scripts run on the server as well, you can view debugging output from the server at <a href="https://substrata.info/script_log">https://substrata.info/script_log</a>.
</p>

<img src="/files/server_script_log.JPG" width="1000px" />

<p>
This page will show the last 1000 or so messages and errors, from all scripts created with your user account.  You must be logged in to view this page.
</p>


<h2>Event Listeners</h2>

<p>
These functions can be defined in your script to listen for certain events.  If you define one of these functions, they will be called when the event triggers for 
the object whose script it is.
</p>
You can also listen for these events on other objects with addEventListener.
</p>


<div class="code-func-definition">onUserTouchedObject(avatar : Avatar, object : Object)</div>

<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">avatar : Avatar</span><span class="code-arg-descrip"> The avatar that touched the object.</span></div>
<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">object : Object</span><span class="code-arg-descrip"> The object that was touched.</span></div>

<div class="code-func-description">
Called when a user's avatar touches an object.
If an avatar continues to touch an object, for example the avatar is standing on an object, this event will continue to be fired at a regular period of something like 0.5 s.
</div>



<div class="code-func-definition">onUserUsedObject(avatar : Avatar, object : Object)</div>

<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">avatar : Avatar</span><span class="code-arg-descrip"> The avatar of the player that used the object.</span></div>
<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">object : Object</span><span class="code-arg-descrip"> The object that was used.</span></div>

<div class="code-func-description">
Called when a user uses an object. By default users press E; a server script can assign a different supported keyboard key, left mouse click, or both with <code>game.setInteractionPrompt</code>. The assigned key/click triggers this same handler, so the script controls the action.
</div>

<h3>Configurable object interaction</h3>
<p>On the server, <code>game.setInteractionPrompt(uid, text, input, visible?)</code> sets the prompt and input for any scripted object. Supported keyboard inputs are letters, digits, <code>f1</code> through <code>f12</code>, <code>space</code>, <code>enter</code>, <code>escape</code>, <code>backspace</code>, <code>delete</code>, <code>pageup</code>, <code>pagedown</code>, <code>home</code>, <code>end</code>, <code>minus</code>, <code>equals</code>, <code>plus</code>, <code>left_bracket</code>, <code>right_bracket</code> and the arrow keys. Use <code>mouse_left</code> for click-only, <code>both</code> for E plus left-click, or a key such as <code>f</code>; append <code>+mouse_left</code> to a key to enable both. The text is custom action text, for example <code>"Open door"</code>. Visibility affects only the label, not whether the input works. Call it again when object state changes to update the text.</p>
<pre class="code-block">
--lua
if IS_SERVER then
  game.setInteractionPrompt(12345, "Open door", "f+mouse_left", true)
end

function onUserUsedObject(avatar, object)
  if IS_SERVER then
    -- Put the object's action here; key and click reach this same handler.
  end
end
</pre>


<h2>Game scripts and focused input</h2>

<p>
An object can act as a small multiplayer game when its Luau script handles input and keeps shared rules on the server. A player looks at the nearby scripted object and presses E to focus it; Escape leaves the game session. Define <code>onUserKeyPressed(avatar, object, input)</code>, <code>onUserKeyReleased(avatar, object, input)</code>, <code>onUserGameAction(avatar, object, action_id, values)</code>, <code>onUserGameMessage(avatar, object, name, payload)</code>, or <code>onUserGameSessionChanged(avatar, object, active)</code> to opt into the focused game session. For a normal button, <code>values</code> is nil; for a form, it is a table keyed by field ID. The server runs these callbacks authoritatively and checks that input belongs to an active, nearby participant.
</p>

<p>
The game APIs in this guide require the matching Metasiberia Linux server release; client presentation and negotiated input/state features also require the updated Qt client. Source code alone does not add tools to an older running server or client. Check <code>get_luau_reference</code> topic <code>game</code> and the connected MCP tool list for the active release before promising that a feature is available in a live world.
</p>

<p>
Each Luau VM is limited to 128 MiB of allocated Lua memory. The server also limits the combined allocation of all owner VMs across all worlds to 1 GiB. When a VM is registered, the remaining server budget is shared across active VMs without lowering any VM's limit below its current allocation. If the total is exhausted, the server refuses to register another VM. A client shares one 128 MiB VM across its loaded object scripts.
</p>

<p>
On the authoritative server, <code>game.getPlayersNear(object, radius)</code> returns active participants whose latest server-stored avatar position is within the radius in metres of the object's world-space bounds centre. The object must be the game host or a live object spawned by this game; radius is 0..1000 metres. The result is capped at 256 participants, and this query shares the game's 60 spatial queries per second budget with object overlap queries. Avatar positions are reported by clients, so this helps implement proximity zones and interactions but is not a physics collision event or an anti-cheat movement check.

For occupancy triggers, <code>game.getPlayersOverlapping(object)</code> returns active game participants whose conservative 0.6-by-0.6-by-1.9 metre avatar AABB intersects the object's world AABB. The object must be the live game host or an object spawned by the same game. This server query helps implement capture zones, finish lines and entry triggers; it uses client-reported avatar positions and is not an exact physics contact or anti-cheat check. It shares the 60-per-second-per-game and 600-per-second-per-world spatial-query budget.
</p>

<p>
For server-side mouse or gamepad targeting, call <code>game.getAimTarget(avatar, max_distance)</code> from an input callback or game loop. It returns <code>{object, object_uid, position, normal, distance}</code> for the nearest live object spawned by this game, or nil when there is no hit. Pass <code>true</code> as the optional third argument to also return the nearest collidable, non-sensor world object, such as an interactive switch; omit it or pass <code>false</code> to keep the result limited to this game's spawned objects. Collidable world-object AABBs block the ray in either mode. Maximum distance is 100 metres. The server chooses the returned object UID, but this does not grant write permission: object property changes still require the script owner to own that object, and transforms recheck parcel placement permission. The view direction is client-reported, so this is not an anti-cheat camera check. The ray uses a server-side 64-metre spatial grid and caps each query at 8,192 object candidates; denser regions return nil and an error. This query is limited to 10 calls per second per game and 200 per second per world, in addition to the spatial-query limits of 60 per second per game and 600 per second per world. Updated clients send the current camera origin and direction in the negotiated game-input snapshot (through the cursor when it is in the viewport, otherwise through screen centre). The server rejects camera origins more than 30 metres from the latest avatar position or outside +/-10,000,000 metres; the view direction remains client-reported and is not an anti-cheat camera check. Older clients fall back to their latest avatar view angles. For player-vs-player targeting, <code>game.getAimPlayerTarget(avatar, max_distance)</code> returns the nearest other live participant in this same game session as <code>{avatar, avatar_uid, position, normal, distance}</code>; collidable world and game objects block the ray. Player hitboxes are conservative 0.6 by 0.6 by 1.9 metre AABBs based on server-stored avatar positions, not exact animated-mesh or physics contacts, and the query shares the 10 aim queries/sec budget. The client-only <code>game.raycast(max_distance)</code> uses the current pointer ray when the pointer is inside the viewport and the screen-centre ray otherwise, matching the ray sent to the server for <code>game.getAimTarget</code>. It returns <code>{object, object_uid, position, normal, distance}</code> for a local physics hit and skips the game host object; use it for presentation only because it does not authorize shared game actions. <code>game.setObjectTransform(object, transform)</code> moves, rotates, or scales a live object spawned by the same game; position is required, and the transform table may include <code>axis</code> plus <code>angle</code>, and <code>scale</code>. The server rechecks parcel placement permission, broadcasts accepted transforms, and allows up to 600 transform updates per second per game and 12,000 per second per world. These are kinematic updates; they do not apply forces or generate physics contacts.
</p>

<p>
For fast scripted movement, <code>game.sweepAABB(object, displacement)</code> checks the object's current world-space AABB along a displacement up to 100 metres. It returns the nearest blocking object, UID, fraction, distance, contact-origin position and axis normal, or nil when the path is clear. A server-side 64-metre spatial grid narrows candidates to collidable world objects and collidable live objects spawned by the same game; sensor and non-collidable objects are ignored. A single query accepts at most 8,192 candidates and returns an error in denser swept regions. The sweep uses conservative AABB bounds, so it can report a hit before the underlying mesh would touch; it does not simulate forces, report a contact manifold, or include avatars. It allows 30 collision queries per second per game and shares a 200-per-second query cap with <code>game.getAimTarget</code>, and also consumes the spatial-query budget of 60 per second per game and 600 per second per world.
</p>

<p>
For ordinary kinematic game-piece movement with wall sliding, <code>game.moveObjectWithSlide(object, displacement)</code> performs up to three conservative AABB sweeps and applies the accepted transform on the server. It returns <code>true, result</code>, where result has <code>position</code>, <code>moved</code>, <code>blocked</code>, and a <code>contacts</code> array containing the contacted object, UID, normal, and leg fraction. It accepts displacements up to 100 metres, uses the same 8,192-candidate cap and collision-query and transform-update budgets as the lower-level APIs. It does not simulate forces or collide with avatars.
</p>

<pre class="code-block">
--lua
local function moveWithSweep(object, displacement)
  local hit, reason = game.sweepAABB(object, displacement)
  if reason then return false, reason end
  local fraction = hit and math.max(0, hit.fraction - 0.001) or 1
  local position = object.pos + displacement * fraction
  return game.setObjectTransform(object, {position=position})
end
</pre>

<pre class="code-block">
--lua
function onUserKeyPressed(avatar, object, input)
  if input ~= "mouse_left" and input ~= "gamepad_a" then return end
  local hit = game.getAimTarget(avatar, 30)
  if hit then
    -- Only this game's live spawned objects can be returned or destroyed.
    game.destroyObject(hit.object)
    game.setHUDText("Target removed", avatar)
  end
end
</pre>

<p>
Focused input includes supported keyboard and mouse buttons (<code>mouse_left</code>, <code>mouse_middle</code>, <code>mouse_right</code>) and Qt gamepad buttons: <code>gamepad_a</code>, <code>gamepad_b</code>, <code>gamepad_x</code>, <code>gamepad_y</code>, <code>gamepad_l1</code>, <code>gamepad_r1</code>, <code>gamepad_l3</code>, <code>gamepad_r3</code>, <code>gamepad_start</code>, <code>gamepad_select</code>, and the D-pad names <code>gamepad_up</code>, <code>gamepad_down</code>, <code>gamepad_left</code>, <code>gamepad_right</code>. <code>onUserKeyReleased(avatar, object, input)</code> reports release, and <code>game.isKeyDown(avatar, input)</code> reads held digital input from the server game loop. <code>game.getInput(avatar)</code> returns the latest focused pointer position as normalized <code>pointer.x</code>/<code>pointer.y</code> values from 0 to 1, plus the connected gamepad's <code>left_x</code>, <code>left_y</code>, <code>right_x</code>, <code>right_y</code>, <code>left_trigger</code> and <code>right_trigger</code> values, and a <code>wheel={x,y}</code> table of scroll steps. Stick and trigger values range from -1 to 1 for sticks and 0 to 1 for triggers. Wheel steps accumulate on the server up to 100 per axis and are consumed once per <code>game.getInput</code> call. The Qt client sends snapshots at up to 30 Hz; the server accepts at most 60 updates per second per participant and 3,600 per second per world, and <code>get_object_script_log</code> reports rejected excess updates. For clients sending capability-gated snapshots, if updates stop for 500 ms, analog/pointer/wheel state becomes neutral, aim rays expire, and held keys read as released. Wheel support requires the negotiated client/server capability.
</p>

<p>
For controls that should work the same across keyboard, mouse and gamepad, register a named action with <code>game.bindAction(name, controls)</code>, such as <code>game.bindAction("jump", {"space", "gamepad_a"})</code>. In the server game loop, <code>game.isActionDown(avatar, "jump")</code> reads whether an active participant is holding any bound control; in the focused client loop, <code>game.isLocalActionDown("jump")</code> is presentation-only. Bindings declared in the script apply to every player. Changing a mapping for one player at runtime requires sending and validating that choice through the game state or RPC, then updating the server mapping as well. See <code>get_luau_reference</code> topic <code>game_bindings</code> for the supported control names and limits.
</p>

<pre class="code-block">
--lua
local score = 0

function onUserKeyPressed(avatar, object, input)
  if input == "mouse_left" or input == "gamepad_a" or input == "space" then
    score += 1
    game.setHUDText("Score: " .. score, avatar)
  end
end
</pre>

<p>Position a text HUD explicitly with <code>game.setHUDText(text, avatar, {x=0, y=0.9})</code>. x is its normalized horizontal center and y its normalized top edge, each in -1..1 screen coordinates. Omit the layout to keep the centered upper-screen default. Explicit placement requires a client and server advertising the text-HUD layout capability.</p>

<pre class="code-block">
--lua
function showChoices(avatar)
  game.setHUDMenu(avatar, "Door control",
    { {id="open", label="Open"}, {id="close", label="Close"} },
    "Choose what the door should do.", {x=-0.58, y=0.82, width=0.42})
end
</pre>

<p>For scoreboards or status panels, pass an optional eighth <code>text_widgets</code> argument to <code>game.setHUDMenu</code>. It is a dense array of up to eight labels. Each label has a unique <code>id</code>, <code>text</code>, normalized horizontal center <code>x</code>, normalized top edge <code>y</code>, and normalized <code>width</code>. Values stay within the screen bounds. Optional <code>text_color</code> and <code>background_color</code> tables use RGB components from 0 to 1; <code>background_alpha</code> controls the card opacity. Example: <code>{id='score', text='Score: 12', x=0, y=0.4, width=0.2, text_color={r=1,g=0.9,b=0.2}, background_color={r=0.04,g=0.05,b=0.08}, background_alpha=0.85}</code>. Text widgets are available when both the Qt client and Linux server advertise <code>SCRIPTED_GAME_HUD_TEXT_WIDGETS</code>; custom colors additionally require <code>SCRIPTED_GAME_HUD_TEXT_STYLE</code>.</p>

<pre class="code-block">
--lua
function showScoreboard(avatar, score, lives)
  game.setHUDMenu(avatar, "Round status", {}, nil, nil, nil, nil, {
    {id="score", text="Score: " .. score, x=-0.72, y=0.88, width=0.24},
    {id="lives", text="Lives: " .. lives, x=0.72, y=0.88, width=0.24}
  })
end
</pre>

<pre class="code-block">
--lua
function askPlayerName(avatar)
  game.setHUDMenu(avatar, "Join game",
    {{id="submit", label="Continue"}}, "Enter a name.",
    nil, {{id="name", label="Player name", required=true}})
end

function onUserGameAction(avatar, object, action_id, values)
  if action_id == "submit" then
    game.setHUDText("Welcome, " .. values.name, avatar)
  end
end
</pre>

<pre class="code-block">
--lua
function showDifficulty(avatar)
  game.setHUDMenu(avatar, "New game", {{id="start", label="Start"}},
    "Choose a difficulty.", nil,
    {{id="difficulty", label="Difficulty", type="select", options={"Easy", "Normal", "Hard"}, required=true}})
end

function onUserGameAction(avatar, object, action_id, values)
  if action_id == "start" then
    -- values.difficulty is one of the declared options; the server checks it.
    game.setHUDText("Starting on " .. values.difficulty, avatar)
  end
end
</pre>

<pre class="code-block">
--lua
-- Keep reusable layouts in this game's script. These entries reference
-- existing objects owned by the game creator; they are not a global library.
local prefabs = {
  gate = {
    {template=wall, offset={x=0, y=0, z=0}},
    {template=wall, offset={x=4, y=0, z=0}, axis={x=0,y=0,z=1}, angle=math.pi/2},
    {template=lamp, offset={x=2, y=0, z=2}, scale={x=0.8,y=0.8,z=0.8}}
  }
}
local spawned, error_message = game.spawnGroup(prefabs.gate, Vec3d(100, 200, 0))
-- Returns all created objects, or nil and an error if the complete group
-- cannot be placed. A call accepts at most 64 entries; each game can create 128 copies per second and keep 1,024 alive.
</pre>

<p>
Games can also create bounded voxel geometry directly from Luau without a pre-placed template. This is useful for boards, block maps and simple game pieces; the object is temporary and follows the game's parcel and object-count limits.
</p>
<pre class="code-block">
--lua
local cells = {
  {x=0,y=0,z=0,material=1}, {x=1,y=0,z=0,material=2},
  {x=0,y=1,z=0,material=2}, {x=1,y=1,z=0,material=1}
}
local piece, error_message = game.createVoxelObject({
  cells=cells,
  position={x=100,y=200,z=1},
  scale={x=0.25,y=0.25,z=0.25},
  collidable=false, -- set true for blocking level geometry
  materials={{r=0.1,g=0.5,b=0.9}, {r=0.9,g=0.35,b=0.1}}
})
if not piece then print("Could not create game piece: " .. tostring(error_message)) end
</pre>
<p>
Each voxel object accepts 1–256 unique cells with integer coordinates from -64 to 64. Use either one shared <code>color={r,g,b}</code> or a <code>materials</code> palette of 1–16 RGB colors; each cell's optional one-based <code>material</code> index selects its palette color and defaults to 1. Scale components range from 0.001 to 10 metres and color channels from 0 to 1. Collision is enabled by default; set <code>collidable=false</code> for decorative voxel objects. The operation runs on the authoritative server, checks the game's owner and parcel permissions, and returns the created object or <code>nil, error</code>. Voxel objects are temporary and are removed when the owning game object is removed. Voxel templates copied by <code>game.spawnObject</code> and <code>game.spawnGroup</code> count against the same per-game and per-world cell budgets.
</p>

<p>
Each <code>spawnGroup</code> entry may include <code>offset={x,y,z}</code> from the group origin, an optional axis-angle rotation using <code>axis={x,y,z}, angle=radians</code> (both must be supplied together; angle is limited to ±2π), and an optional absolute instance <code>scale={x,y,z}</code> with positive components from 0.01 to 100. Omitting these fields preserves the template transform. One call accepts at most 64 entries and places the complete group only if every template, permission and resource limit check succeeds.
</p>

<p>
On the authoritative server, <code>game.getOverlaps(object)</code> returns up to 256 other live objects spawned by the same game whose world-space axis-aligned bounds intersect the supplied game object; if more than 256 objects overlap, it returns an error instead of a partial list. Call it from the server game loop to implement broad-phase hit detection for game pieces. It scans only the bounded spawned-object set owned by this game and is limited to 60 queries per second per game and 600 per second across the world. This is an AABB overlap query, not a physics contact event: it does not report contact normals, exact mesh intersections, avatars, or objects from other games. <code>game.getOverlapChanges(object)</code> returns <code>{entered, exited}</code> arrays of live game objects whose AABB overlap began or ended since the previous query for that object. Its snapshots are limited to 16,384 overlap pairs per game and it shares the 60-query-per-second game limit and the 600-per-second world limit; a query that exceeds the snapshot limit returns <code>nil, error</code> without changing the prior snapshot.
</p>

<p>
Focused client scripts may call <code>game.setCamera(position, look_at, horizontal_fov_degrees?, transition_seconds?)</code>. The optional horizontal field of view is 30–120 degrees; omitting it keeps the current value. The optional transition duration is 0–10 seconds; zero or omission moves immediately, while a positive duration smoothly interpolates position, shortest-path heading, pitch and field of view. A new camera command replaces an in-progress transition from the current camera pose. The game temporarily owns the local camera while focused, and the original camera settings are restored on release or when the game session ends. Camera control is local presentation and does not authorize server-side actions.
</p>

<p>
This requires the updated Qt client and server support for focused game sessions. While its object owns local E-focus and the standard avatar camera is active, a client script can call <code>game.setPlayerControl(move_x, move_y, jump?, run?)</code> from <code>game.createClientLoop</code>. The two movement axes are finite values from -1 to 1, where positive y means forward and positive x means right; diagonal input is normalized. <code>game.getLocalInput()</code> returns the focused player's keyboard/gamepad movement as <code>{available, move_x, move_y, jump, run, w, a, s, d}</code> and a sparse <code>keys</code> table containing currently held supported keys (for example <code>input.keys.space</code>, <code>input.keys.escape</code>, <code>input.keys.left</code>, or <code>input.keys.e</code>). It also returns <code>pointer={x,y}</code> normalized to 0..1 from the top-left when the cursor is in the viewport (otherwise nil), <code>mouse={left,middle,right}</code> held-button booleans, and <code>gamepad={connected,left_x,left_y,right_x,right_y,left_trigger,right_trigger,a,b,x,y,l1,r1,l3,r3,start,select,up,down,left,right}</code>. Stick axes range from -1 to 1 and triggers from 0 to 1. The local snapshot is unavailable if the game does not own focus or the viewport has lost keyboard focus. The setter returns <code>true, ""</code> when accepted, or <code>false, error</code> when the game is not focused, the player is in a vehicle, or the camera is in another mode. Refresh the command each loop tick: the client expires it after 0.35 seconds, on focus loss, or when the loop stops. Accepted control feeds the local collision-aware avatar controller and takes precedence over physical movement for that frame. It does not make avatar movement server-authoritative, so do not use it to decide competitive outcomes or trusted shared state; send intent to a server callback and validate game rules there.
</p>
<pre class="code-block">
--lua
-- Declare this server callback so a nearby player can press E and focus the game.
function onUserKeyPressed(avatar, object, key)
  if IS_SERVER then
    -- Handle shared actions here, or leave this handler empty.
  end
end

-- Client-only local movement controller. Server game rules belong in createLoop.
if IS_CLIENT then
  game.createClientLoop(function(object, dt)
    local input = game.getLocalInput()
    if input and input.available then
      local ok, err = game.setPlayerControl(input.move_x, input.move_y, input.jump, input.run)
    end
  end, 20)
end
</pre>

<p>
<code>game.createClientLoop</code> runs at 1–30 Hz while the game owns E-focus. The Qt client handles at most 16 due game-loop events per rendered frame, with up to four catch-up steps for each event (64 callbacks maximum per frame). Remaining due events stay queued. Each Luau callback is also limited to 10,000 VM interrupts. These are count limits; callbacks still run on the Qt event thread while holding world state, so keep them short and avoid blocking operations.
</p>

<p>
The game API also provides server loops, player lists, a text HUD and up to four independent named interactive menus per player and game. Each menu has a title, optional description, up to eight action buttons, and normalized screen placement. Pass <code>{id="inventory", x=-0.7, y=0.8, width=0.3}</code> as the fifth argument to <code>game.setHUDMenu</code>; omit <code>id</code> for the default <code>main</code> panel. An optional sixth argument defines up to four fields: text input such as <code>{id="name", label="Name", required=true}</code>, a bounded selector such as <code>{id="difficulty", label="Difficulty", type="select", options={"Easy", "Normal", "Hard"}}</code>, or a numeric input such as <code>{id="damage", label="Damage", type="number", min=0, max=100}</code>, or a checkbox such as <code>{id="confirm", label="Confirm", type="checkbox", checked=false}</code>. Numeric and checkbox fields require a new server release; older capable clients show text inputs while the server validates submissions. Checkbox values are the strings <code>"true"</code> and <code>"false"</code>; a required checkbox must be checked. Select fields need 2–8 unique options, each 1–64 bytes. Each button may optionally include its own screen bounds, for example <code>{id="start", label="Start", layout={x=0, y=0.2, width=0.3, height=0.08}}</code>. The button layout's x is its normalized horizontal center, y is its normalized top edge, width is relative to the full screen width, and height is relative to the vertical UI extent; x/y use -1..1, width 0.05..1 and height 0.025..0.5, with the button kept inside the screen. Buttons without layout use the automatic grid. This requires a client and server that advertise the action-layout capability. Clicking a button submits values to <code>onUserGameAction(avatar, object, action_id, values)</code>; each value is limited to 256 bytes, required fields are checked by the server, and select values must match a declared option. Action IDs must be unique across open panels. <code>game.clearHUDMenu(avatar, id)</code> clears one panel; omitting the ID clears <code>main</code>. The API also provides transient state snapshots, versioned persistent game/player data, authorized object spawning, and bounded client-to-server messages. Server scripts can read and write the shared snapshot with <code>game.getState()</code> and <code>game.setState(table)</code>; each snapshot is limited to 16 KiB and all active game snapshots in one world share an 8 MiB budget; updates are limited to 10 per second per game and 600 per second per world. Focused clients receive that snapshot on join and may read the latest one with <code>game.getState()</code>; the callback <code>onGameStateChanged(object, version, state)</code> always receives a reconstructed full state. When client and server advertise the state-delta capability, updates whose byte representation benefits use a bounded versioned prefix/suffix delta on the wire. The server sends periodic full checkpoints, while late joiners and older clients always get full snapshots. A focused client script may call <code>game.sendToServer(name, plain_table)</code>; it returns true when queued and false when unsupported, unfocused, over the rate/size limit, or not serializable. Names are 1–32 ASCII identifier characters, payloads are at most 4 KiB, and the server independently enforces 20 messages per second per participant/game and 1,200 per second per world. It invokes <code>onUserGameMessage(avatar, object, name, payload)</code> only for an active nearby game participant; <code>get_object_script_log</code> reports cumulative <code>rejected_game_rpc_messages</code>, <code>rejected_game_input_updates</code>, and <code>rejected_game_events</code> counts for server rate-limit rejections. The server rechecks the session and plain payload shape; a client message does not authorize a world mutation. The API is still experimental: it does not expose arbitrary custom HUD widgets, a built-in global prefab editor or spawned-object pooling, or server-authoritative avatar movement and collision events. Scripts can make per-game persistent template catalogs with the storage and atomic spawn APIs described below. Keep competitive rules and state changes on the server; local raycasts, camera control, and player control do not authorize shared actions.
</p>

<p>
Use the seventh argument to <code>game.setHUDMenu</code> for up to four progress bars, for example <code>{{id="health", label="Health", value=75, max=100}}</code>. Values must be finite and satisfy <code>0 &lt;= value &lt;= max &lt;= 1,000,000,000</code>. The optional eighth argument adds up to eight individually positioned text widgets, for example <code>{{id="score", text="Score: 12", x=0, y=0.4, width=0.2}}</code>. Each widget uses normalized screen-center x and top y coordinates in -1..1, a width in 0.05..0.8, and 1–256 bytes of text; its horizontal bounds must stay on screen. A menu can contain text widgets, progress bars, form fields and actions together. Progress bars and text widgets require the updated Linux server and Qt client; older clients retain any menu actions they support and omit unsupported elements.
</p>

<p>
Scripts can build a persistent, per-game prefab catalog from template object UIDs using <code>game.getData</code>, <code>game.setData</code>, <code>findObjectForUID</code>, and <code>game.spawnGroup</code>. Save the source UID and local offset, rotation, and scale for each part; resolve UIDs again when spawning and handle deleted templates. The catalog survives script reloads and server restarts as long as its template objects remain in the world. It is script-managed game data, not a global editor library or a pool of already spawned objects. Storage is limited to 16 KiB per record, 256 KiB and 64 records per game, and 10 writes per second; an atomic group spawn accepts 1–64 entries. A game can own up to 1,024 spawned objects, the world up to 32,768 total; creation is limited to 128 objects per second per game and 1,024 per second per world. See <code>get_luau_reference</code> topic <code>prefabs</code> for a complete example.
</p>

<p>
To show the standard blue materialisation effect on an existing avatar without moving it, call <code>game.playAvatarSpawnEffect(avatar)</code> from an authoritative server script. Pass a live avatar table from an event handler or <code>game.getPlayers()</code>. The server broadcasts the request to capable clients; each client runs the same two-second effect used when an avatar appears. This requires a rebuilt server and capable Qt clients.

For a one-shot cue, call <code>game.emitEvent(name)</code> on the authoritative server. Capable participants focused when the event is emitted receive it in order through <code>onGameEvent(object, name, sequence, server_time)</code>; unlike game-state snapshots, accepted events are not coalesced. When both peers negotiate timestamp support, <code>server_time</code> is the synchronized world clock at emission; older compatible peers receive 0. Delivery is limited to 20 events per game per second and 256 focused participants per event; a world may emit at most 1,200 events and 12,000 total recipient deliveries per second. Events use the reliable TCP connection while it remains active. Late joiners and reconnecting players do not receive old events; older clients without the negotiated capability skip them. The call returns false when any per-game, world event-rate or recipient-delivery limit is reached. This needs an updated server and clients that advertise the game-event capability. Audio and animation playback still runs locally, and the referenced sound or exact animation clip must already be loaded by each client. To align a looping clip's phase across clients, compute <code>math.max(0, getCurrentTime() - server_time)</code> and pass it as the second Lua argument after the clip name to <code>object:startPlayingAnimation(name, elapsed_seconds)</code>. This aligns the local clip clock; it does not synchronize audio or guarantee identical render frames.
</p>

<pre class="code-block">
--lua
local last_cue_sequence = nil

function onUserGameSessionChanged(avatar, object, active)
  if not IS_SERVER then return end
  if active then
    game.setHUDMenu(avatar, "Cues", {{id="fire", label="Play cue"}})
  else
    game.clearHUDMenu(avatar)
  end
end

function onUserGameAction(avatar, object, action_id)
  if not IS_SERVER or action_id ~= "fire" then return end
  game.emitEvent("fire")
end

function onGameEvent(object, name, sequence, server_time)
  if not IS_CLIENT or name ~= "fire" then return end
  if last_cue_sequence ~= nil and sequence <= last_cue_sequence then return end
  last_cue_sequence = sequence
  object:playAudio()
  local clip = object:getAnimationIndex("Shoot")
  if clip >= 0 then
    if server_time > 0 then
      local elapsed = math.min(3600, math.max(0, getCurrentTime() - server_time))
      object:startPlayingAnimation(clip, elapsed)
    else
      object:startPlayingAnimation(clip)
    end
  end
end
</pre>


<div class="code-func-definition">onUserMovedNearToObject(avatar : Avatar, object : Object)</div>

<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">avatar : Avatar</span><span class="code-arg-descrip"> The avatar that moved near the object.</span></div>
<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">object : Object</span><span class="code-arg-descrip"> The object that the player moved near to.</span></div>

<div class="code-func-description">
Called when a user avatar moves within 20 metres of an object. The server checks the transition against its latest stored avatar position and runs the callback once per entry. Avatar movement is still reported by the client, so this does not provide authoritative movement validation.
</div>



<div class="code-func-definition">onUserMovedAwayFromObject(avatar : Avatar, object : Object)</div>

<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">avatar : Avatar</span><span class="code-arg-descrip"> The avatar that moved near the object.</span></div>
<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">object : Object</span><span class="code-arg-descrip"> The object that the player moved away from.</span></div>

<div class="code-func-description">
Called when a user avatar moves 20 metres or farther from an object after a previously accepted near transition. The server checks and deduplicates the exit. Avatar movement is still reported by the client, so this does not provide authoritative movement validation.
</div>


<div class="code-func-definition">onUserEnteredParcel(avatar : Avatar, object : Object, parcel : Parcel)</div>

<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">avatar : Avatar</span><span class="code-arg-descrip"> The avatar that entered the parcel.</span></div>
<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">object : Object</span><span class="code-arg-descrip"> The object in the parcel.</span></div>
<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">parcel : Parcel</span><span class="code-arg-descrip"> The parcel.</span></div>

<div class="code-func-description">
Called when a user avatar enters the land parcel that the script object is inside.
</div>


<div class="code-func-definition">onUserExitedParcel(avatar : Avatar, object : Object, parcel : Parcel)</div>

<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">avatar : Avatar</span><span class="code-arg-descrip"> The avatar that exited the parcel.</span></div>
<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">object : Object</span><span class="code-arg-descrip"> The object in the parcel.</span></div>
<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">parcel : Parcel</span><span class="code-arg-descrip"> The parcel.</span></div>

<div class="code-func-description">
Called when a user avatar exits the land parcel that the script object is inside.
</div>


<div class="code-func-definition">onUserEnteredVehicle(avatar : Avatar, vehicle_ob : Object)</div>

<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">avatar : Avatar</span><span class="code-arg-descrip"> The avatar that entered the vehicle.</span></div>
<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">vehicle_ob : Object</span><span class="code-arg-descrip"> The vehicle object</span></div>

<div class="code-func-description">
Called when a user avatar enters (or sits on in the case of a bike) a vehicle.
</div>


<div class="code-func-definition">onUserExitedVehicle(avatar : Avatar, vehicle_ob : Object)</div>

<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">avatar : Avatar</span><span class="code-arg-descrip"> The avatar that exited the vehicle.</span></div>
<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">vehicle_ob : Object</span><span class="code-arg-descrip"> The vehicle object</span></div>

<div class="code-func-description">
Called when a user avatar exits a vehicle.
</div>



<h2>Global functions</h2>


<div class="code-func-definition">getObjectForUID(uid : number) : Object</div>

<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">uid : number</span><span class="code-arg-descrip"> The UID (unique identifier) of the object.</span></div>

<div class="code-func-description">
Returns the object that has the given UID.  Throws exception if no such object exists.
</div>



<div class="code-func-definition">showMessageToUser(msg : String, av : Avatar)</div>

<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">msg : String</span><span class="code-arg-descrip">The message to send to the avatar's user.</span></div>
<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">av : Avatar</span><span class="code-arg-descrip">The avatar of the user to send the message to.</span></div>

<div class="code-func-description">
Shows a string message on the screen of the user of the given avatar.  The message will be displayed for a few seconds.
</div>



<div class="code-func-definition">createTimer(onTimerEvent : function, interval_time_s : number, repeating : bool) : number</div>

<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">onTimerEvent : function</span><span class="code-arg-descrip">The timer event handler to be called when the timer fires.</span></div>
<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">interval_time_s : number</span><span class="code-arg-descrip">The time interval, in seconds, until the timer fires.</span></div>
<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">repeating : bool</span><span class="code-arg-descrip">Should the timer keep firing every interval_time_s seconds, or should it just fire once.</span></div>

<div class="code-func-description">
<p>
Creates a timer, when the timer interval is complete, onTimerEvent is called.   onTimerEvent should be a function with the following signature:
</p>
<p>
onTimerEvent(object : Object)
</p>
<p>
Throws an exception if more than 4 timer events are created without being destroyed.
</p>
<p>
The interval must be finite and no greater than 86400 seconds; values below 0.1 seconds are clamped to 0.1. Server timers run on the authoritative server, which dispatches at most 64 due timer events per roughly 100 ms poll; events over that limit remain queued. Fixed-step server loops retain up to 5 seconds of pending simulation time, process at most four steps per dispatch, then catch up on later polls. Client timers run locally without requiring E-game focus, with a shared limit of 64 callbacks and 256 due-event removals per rendered frame; use them for presentation such as NPC animation selection, and keep shared game rules on the server.
</p>
<p>
Returns a timer handle that can be passed to destroyTimer.
</p>
</div>



<div class="code-func-definition">destroyTimer(timer_handle : number) : number</div>

<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">timer_handle : number)</span><span class="code-arg-descrip">A timer handle that was previously returned from createTimer.</span></div>

<div class="code-func-description">
<p>
Destroys a timer.  Has no effect if the timer has already completed (for non-repeating timers), or has already been destroyed.
</p>
</div>



<div class="code-func-definition">addEventListener(event_name : string, ob_uid : number, handler : Function) : number</div>

<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">event_name : string</span><span class="code-arg-descrip">The name of the event.  Valid event names are 
"onUserUsedObject", "onUserTouchedObject", "onUserMovedNearToObject", "onUserMovedAwayFromObject", "onUserEnteredParcel", "onUserExitedParcel", "onUserEnteredVehicle", "onUserExitedVehicle"</span></div>

<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">ob_uid : number</span><span class="code-arg-descrip">UID of the object that the event will be listened on.  For example, for onUserTouchedObject, this is the UID of the object
that will have to be touched to trigger the touch event.</span></div>

<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">handler : Function</span><span class="code-arg-descrip">A function that will be called when the event occurs.  You can use
your existing event handling functions such as onUserTouchedObject.</span></div>

<div class="code-func-description">
<p>
Adds a listener for an event on another object. 
</p>
<p>For example, this code listens for the touch event on the object with UID 583:</p>
<pre class="code-block">
addEventListener("onUserTouchedObject", 583, onUserTouchedObject)
</pre>

</div>



<div class="code-func-definition">doHTTPGetRequestAsync(URL : string, additional_header_lines : table, onDone : function, onError : function)</div>

<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">URL : string</span><span class="code-arg-descrip">The URL to request.  Should be a http or https URL.</span></div>

<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">additional_header_lines : table</span>
<span class="code-arg-descrip">A table with any additional header lines to send in the HTTP request.</span></div>

<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">onDone : function</span><span class="code-arg-descrip">
A function that will be called when the HTTP request has completed.  onDone takes a single argument which is a table like so:

<pre class="code-block">
{
  response_code: number
  response_message : string
  mime_type : string
  body_data : buffer
}
</pre>

<p>response_code is the HTTP response code, e.g. 200 or 404.</p>
<p>response_message is the corresponding string message such as "OK" or "File not found".</p>
<p>mime_type is the content type of the response, and will be something like "text/html".</p>
<p>body_data is the main body of the response.</p>
</span></div>

<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">onError : function</span><span class="code-arg-descrip">
A function that will be called if an error is encountered while doing the HTTP request, 
for example a connection cannot be made to the server.
onError takes a single argument which is a table like so:

<pre class="code-block">
{
  error_code : number
  error_description : string
}
</pre>

Current error codes are

<pre class="code-block">
ErrorCode_OK = 0,
ErrorCode_Other = 1,
ErrorCode_RateLimited = 2
</pre>


</span></div>

<div class="code-func-description">
<p>
Sends an HTTP request to a server.
</p>
<P>Requests are rate-limited: a maximum of 5 requests per 300 seconds are allowed per user.  Exceeding this rate will result in
onError being called with error_code = 2 (ErrorCode_RateLimited).
</P>
<p>For example:</p>
<pre class="code-block">

function onDone(result)
	print("response_code: " .. tostring(result.response_code))
	print("response_message: " .. result.response_message)
	print("mime_type: " .. result.mime_type)
	print("body_data: " .. result.body_data)
end

function onError(result)
	print("error_code: " .. tostring(result.error_code))
	print("error_description: " .. result.error_description)
end

doHTTPGetRequestAsync(
	"https://coolwebsite.com/someapi?id=dfdfsdf", -- URL
	{ Authorization = "Basic afsdefsdfd" } -- additional_header_lines
	onDone, onError
)
</pre>

</div>







<div class="code-func-definition" id="doHTTPPostRequestAsync">doHTTPPostRequestAsync(URL : string, post_content : string, content_type : string, additional_header_lines : table, onDone : function, onError : function)</div>

<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">URL : string</span><span class="code-arg-descrip">The URL to request.  Should be a http or https URL.</span></div>
<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">post_content : string</span><span class="code-arg-descrip">The main body content of the post request.</span></div>
<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">content_type : string</span><span class="code-arg-descrip">The conent type of the post body, e.g. .</span></div>

<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">additional_header_lines : table</span>
<span class="code-arg-descrip">A table with any additional header lines to send in the HTTP request.</span></div>

<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">onDone : function</span><span class="code-arg-descrip">
A function that will be called when the HTTP request has completed.  onDone takes a single argument which is a table like so:

<pre class="code-block">
{
  response_code: number
  response_message : string
  mime_type : string
  body_data : buffer
}
</pre>

<p>response_code is the HTTP response code, e.g. 200 or 404.</p>
<p>response_message is the corresponding string message such as "OK" or "File not found".</p>
<p>mime_type is the content type of the response, and will be something like "text/html".</p>
<p>body_data is the main body of the response.</p>
</span></div>

<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">onError : function</span><span class="code-arg-descrip">
A function that will be called if an error is encountered while doing the HTTP request, 
for example a connection cannot be made to the server.
onError takes a single argument which is a table like so:

<pre class="code-block">
{
  error_code : number
  error_description : string
}
</pre>

Current error codes are

<pre class="code-block">
ErrorCode_OK = 0,
ErrorCode_Other = 1,
ErrorCode_RateLimited = 2
</pre>


</span></div>

<div class="code-func-description">
<p>
Sends an HTTP post request to a server.
</p>
<P>Requests are rate-limited: a maximum of 5 requests per 300 seconds are allowed per user.  Exceeding this rate will result in
onError being called with error_code = 2 (ErrorCode_RateLimited).
</P>
<p>For example:</p>
<pre class="code-block">

function onDone(result)
	print("response_code: " .. tostring(result.response_code))
	print("response_message: " .. result.response_message)
	print("mime_type: " .. result.mime_type)
	print("body_data: " .. result.body_data)
end

function onError(result)
	print("error_code: " .. tostring(result.error_code))
	print("error_description: " .. result.error_description)
end

doHTTPPostRequestAsync(
	"https://coolwebsite.com/someapi?id=dfdfsdf", -- URL
	"{ id : \"123\" }", -- post content
	"application/json", -- content type
	{ Authorization = "Basic afsdefsdfd" } -- additional_header_lines
	onDone, onError
)
</pre>

</div>










<div class="code-func-definition" id="getSecret">getSecret(secret_name : string) : string</div>

<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">secret_name : string</span><span class="code-arg-descrip">
The name of the secret to get.</span></div>

<div class="code-func-description">
<p>
Get a secret string that has been set via the Account Secrets page (<a href="/secrets">/secrets</a>).
This is useful for retrieving things like API keys that you want to use in a script, but don't want other users to see.
</p>
</div>


<pre class="code-block">
my_api_key = getSecret("MY_API_KEY")
</pre>



<div class="code-func-definition" id="parseJSON">parseJSON(json : string) : string</div>

<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">json : string</span><span class="code-arg-descrip">
A JSON string to parse.</span></div>

<div class="code-func-description">
<p>
Parse a JSON string and convert it to the equivalent Lua object.
</p>
<p>JSON objects and arrays are converted to Lua tables.  JSON null is converted to Lua nil.
</p>
</div>

<pre class="code-block">
parseJSON('[123.456, true, \"abc\", null]') 
-- returns the Lua array table {123.456, true, "abc", nil}
</pre>

<div class="code-func-definition" id="objectstorage_setItem">objectstorage.setItem(key : string, value : Any serialisable Lua value) : string</div>

<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">key : string</span><span class="code-arg-descrip">
The storage item key.  Storage items are uniquely identified by the combination of the script object UID, and the key string.</span></div>

<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">value : Any serialisable Lua value</span><span class="code-arg-descrip">
The value to store.  Can be a single Lua value like a number, or a Lua table.</span></div>


<div class="code-func-description">
<p>
Store a value in object storage.  These values are persistently stored, and remain stored even over server restarts or script reloads and changes, unlike variables in Lua scripts.
</p>
<p>
Overwrites any existing values with the given key and script object.
</p>

<p>For example, here is how you could store a visitor counter in object storage:</p>
<pre class="code-block">
objectstorage.setItem("num_visits", new_num_visits)
</pre>

</div>


<div class="code-func-definition" id="objectstorage_getItem">objectstorage.getItem(key : string) : string</div>

<div class="code-arg"><span class="code-arg-code">key : string</span><span class="code-arg-descrip">
The storage item key.  Storage items are uniquely identified by the combination of the script object UID, and the key string.</span></div>


<div class="code-func-description">
<p>
Retrieves a value from object storage.  Returns nil if there is no such item stored.
</p>

<pre class="code-block">
num_visits = objectstorage.getItem("num_visits")
</pre>

</div>




<h2>Global variables</h2>


<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">this_object : Object</span>
<span class="code-attr-descrip">The object on which the script currently being executed is defined.</span>
</div>

<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">IS_CLIENT : boolean</span>
<span class="code-attr-descrip">True if the script is running on the Substrata client, false if running on the server.</span>
</div>

<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">IS_SERVER : boolean</span>
<span class="code-attr-descrip">True if the script is running on the Substrata server, false if running on the client.</span>
</div>


<h2>Classes</h2>

<div class="code-func-definition">Object</div>

<p>
Attributes:
</p>

<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">model_url : string</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">The Substrata URL of the 3d model for the object</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">pos : Vec3d</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">The position of the object</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">axis : Vec3f</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">The axis of rotation of the object</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">angle : number</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">The counter-clockwise angle of rotation of the object around axis, in radians.</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">scale : Vec3f</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">The scale factor in x, y, and z directions.</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">collidable : boolean</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">Is this object physically collidable with?  
If false, avatars and physics objects will pass through it without colliding with it.</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">dynamic : boolean</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">Is this object a dynamic physics object?</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">sensor : boolean</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">Sensor objects still emit touch events, but objects can pass through them.</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">content : string</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">Content field.  For Text and Hypercard object types, 
this is the text that is displayed.</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">video_autoplay : boolean</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">If true, video is automatically played for Video
object types.</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">video_loop : boolean</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">If true, video automatically loops back to the beginning
and starts playing again.  If false, just plays once.</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">video_muted : boolean</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">If true, video is muted when playing.</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">mass : number</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">Object mass in kg for physics engine.  Only relevant
when dynamic is true.</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">friction : number</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">Object friction. 
Friction of the physics body (dimensionless number, usually between 0 and 1, 0 = no friction, 1 = friction force equals force that presses the two bodies together).</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">restitution : number</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">Restitution of body (dimensionless number, usually between 0 and 1, 0 = completely inelastic collision response, 1 = completely elastic collision response).</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">centre_of_mass_offset_os : Vec3f</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">Offset of centre of mass of object, in object coordinates.  Can be used to e.g. give a vehicle a lower centre of mass than by default.</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">audio_source_url : string</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">
Substrata URL of the audio source for this object.   Can be a mp3 or wav URL</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">audio_volume : number</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">
Volume factor for audio source.  Default is 1.</span></div>

<p>
Client-side object methods include <code>playAudio()</code>, which restarts the loaded source from the beginning, <code>pauseAudio()</code>, which pauses playback at its current position, <code>resumeAudio()</code>, which continues paused playback, and <code>isPlayingAudio()</code>. For animated models, <code>getAnimationIndex(name)</code> returns a clip index or -1, <code>startPlayingAnimation(index_or_name, elapsed_seconds?)</code> cross-fades to a clip by exact name or valid integer index; the optional finite phase offset is limited to 0..3600 seconds. <code>stopPlayingAnimation()</code>/<code>resumePlayingAnimation()</code> pause and resume the current clip at its current pose. These methods return true when the loaded model supports the requested operation. Playback remains local; timestamped game events can align looping clip phase across clients, but do not replicate audio, clip availability or rendered frames.
</p>

<p>
For any loaded GLB/BMesh with a preserved hierarchy, client scripts can inspect and pose nodes on the original model object without splitting it into separate world objects. <code>getNodeCount()</code>, <code>getNodeIndex(name)</code> and <code>getNodeInfo(index_or_name)</code> expose zero-based node indices, parent links, bind-pose translation/quaternion/scale and whether a node is a bone. <code>getBoneCount()</code>/<code>getBoneInfo(bone_index)</code> enumerate the skin joints. <code>setNodeTransform(index_or_name, {translation=Vec3f(...), rotation={x=...,y=...,z=...,w=...}, scale=Vec3f(...)})</code> applies a local post-transform after the authored pose or active clip; omitted values use zero translation, identity rotation and unit scale. <code>clearNodeTransform(index_or_name)</code> removes that override. <code>getAnimationCount()</code> and <code>getAnimationName(index)</code> enumerate loaded clips; existing start/stop/resume methods control playback. This is client-side presentation, not a server-replicated bone state or a moving collision shape. For multiplayer, send validated game state/events and apply the same local pose on capable clients. Static GLB scene transforms are preserved for models with at most 256 rigid mesh nodes; models beyond the renderer's joint limit retain the static import path.
</p>

<p>
Methods:
</p>

<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">getNumMaterials() : number</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">
Returns the number of materials of the object.</span></div>

<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">getMaterial(mat_index : number) : Material</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">
Returns the material of the object with the given index.</span></div>

<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">playAudio()</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">
Restarts the object's loaded audio source from the beginning. Client-side presentation only.</span></div>

<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">pauseAudio()</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">
Pauses local playback at the current position. Use <code>resumeAudio()</code> to continue.</span></div>

<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">resumeAudio()</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">
Continues local playback from the paused position.</span></div>

<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">isPlayingAudio() : boolean</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">
Returns whether the object's audio source is currently playing.</span></div>

<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">getAnimationIndex(name : string) : number</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">
Returns the model animation clip index, or -1 when it is missing. Client-side presentation only.</span></div>

<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">startPlayingAnimation(index_or_name : number | string, elapsed_seconds? : number) : boolean</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">
Cross-fades the local model to the exact clip name or a valid integer index. Optional elapsed_seconds sets the initial loop phase in 0..3600 seconds, useful with the server_time argument of onGameEvent. Returns false if the model or clip is unavailable.</span></div>

<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">stopPlayingAnimation() : boolean</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">
Pauses the current local animation in its current pose. Returns false if the model has no loaded animation.</span></div>

<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">resumePlayingAnimation() : boolean</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">
Resumes the current local animation from its paused pose. Returns false if the model has no loaded animation.</span></div>

<pre class="code-block">
--lua
-- Client presentation for an animated WorldObject that has no separate idle clip.
if IS_CLIENT then
  local previous_position = this_object.pos
  local paused = false
  createTimer(function(object)
    local position = object.pos
    local dx = position.x - previous_position.x
    local dy = position.y - previous_position.y
    local dz = position.z - previous_position.z
    local moving = dx * dx + dy * dy + dz * dz > 0.01
    if moving and paused then
      object:resumePlayingAnimation()
      paused = false
    elseif not moving and not paused then
      object:stopPlayingAnimation()
      paused = true
    end
    previous_position = position
  end, 0.2, true)
end
</pre>

<p>Use this for an animated world object. NPC avatars use the bot's animation slots and avatar animation controller; a script attached to an unrelated object does not control an avatar's skeleton.</p>





<div class="code-func-definition">Material</div>

<p>
Attributes:
</p>

<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">colour : Vec3f</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">The reflective colour of the object.  Vec3f(1,1,1) is a white object.  Non-linear sRGB.</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">colour_texture_url : string</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">The URL of the colour texture.</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">emission_rgb : Vec3f</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">The colour of the emitted light from the object.  Non-linear sRGB.</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">emission_texture_url : string</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">The URL of the emission texture.</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">normal_map_url : string</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">The URL of the normal map.</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">roughness_val : number</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">The roughness of the material. 0 = perfectly smooth, 1 = very rough.</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">roughness_texture_url : string</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">
URL of the metallic-roughness texture.  The metallic value is read from the blue channel, the roughness value is read from the green channel.</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">metallic_fraction_val : number</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">The metallic fraction of the material. 0 = not metal.  1 = completely metal.</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">opacity_val : number</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">
How opaque (non-transparent) the material is.  
Currently this value just determines if the transparent shader is used - it's used in the case where opacity_val &lt; 1.</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">tex_matrix : Matrix2f</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">The texture coordinate matrix.  Default value is {1, 0, 0, 1}.</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">emission_lum_flux_or_lum : number</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">
For the spotlight object materials, this is luminous flux.  For generic model materials, luminance.</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">hologram : boolean</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">Is this a hologram material?
Hologram materials don't block light, but just emit it.</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">double_sided : boolean</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">Should we render back-faces of the triangles with this material?
Rendering is faster when not rendering back-faces, but if your object triangle mesh is not fully sealed, it may result in visible gaps.</span></div>






<div class="code-func-definition">Avatar</div>

<p>
Attributes:
</p>

<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">pos : Vec3d</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">The position of the avatar.  
This is approximately the position of the eyes of the avatar for a standard avatar height, and will be 1.67 m above the surface the avatar is standing on.</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">name : string</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">The name of the user who is controlling the avatar.</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">linear_velocity : Vec3f</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">The linear velocity of the avatar, in metres per second.</span></div>
<div class="code-attr"><span class="code-attr-code">vehicle_inside : Object</span><span class="code-attr-descrip">The vehicle object the avatar is inside-of/riding, or nil if none.</span></div>




<h2>Examples</h2>

<p>
Here is a very simple script that shows a message to a user when they touch an object:
</p>

<pre class="code-block">
--lua

function onUserTouchedObject(av : Avatar, ob : Object)
	showMessageToUser("Hi " .. av.name .. ", you touched the red cube!", av)
end
</pre>
<p>
Which has this result in-world when you walk into the red cube:
</p>
<img src="/files/touched_cube.JPG" width="1000px" />



